== 4. Tychē Points
#columns(2, gutter: 6mm)[
#strong[Spend 1 Tychē Point to:]

- #strong[Invoke] --- reroll all dice, or add +2 after rolling. This works with any tag or Truth you can justify, even one belonging to the scene or an enemy. Invokes are not limited by the tag cap. Invoke before the roll's outcome is resolved.
- #strong[Declare] --- establish a minor fact about the scene. The GM may require it to tie to one of your tags.
- #strong[Resist harder] --- reduce one Resistance cost to 0 (§6).

#strong[Declarations stay minor.] A declared fact can make an approach possible or supply a relevant asset. It can't resolve an action by itself, buy status tiers or Disposition, or change Truths above scene scale (§8).

#strong[Resisting harder.] The Resistance still resolves normally, but its pool cost is 0, so it can't run the pool dry, even if the pool is already empty (§6.1). It doesn't restore spent points or clear an existing running-dry status, and it can't Resist ordinary Disposition loss or a protected payment (§10.7).

Scripted conflicts add timing limits to all three spends (§10.18).

#strong[Earn Tychē Points by:]

- #strong[Accepting a compel] --- the GM or another player offers a meaningful complication based on one of your tags (power, weakness or Scar), Beliefs (§3.5) or your Instinct. Accept → +1 Tychē Point. Refuse → pay 1 Tychē Point.
- #strong[Compelling yourself] --- propose the same kind of complication on yourself. If the GM agrees it meets the rules below, take the complication and gain +1 Tychē Point. Facing it can also earn XP (§12.1).

#strong[What makes a compel]

- #strong[Meaningful.] The complication must change the situation: an obstacle, obligation, exposure, hard choice, or lost opportunity. Flavour or a minor inconvenience doesn't count.
- #strong[Clear offer.] Before you accept, know which tag, Belief, or Instinct justifies it, what will happen, and what it commits your character to. You otherwise control how your character responds.
- #strong[Limits.] A compel must follow from the established meaning of the tag, Belief, or Instinct. It adds no numerical penalty and can't undo a resolved success. An existing penalty doesn't earn Tychē just because a tag, Belief, or Instinct explains it.
- #emph[Feared duellist:] a rival challenges you before an audience whose support you need. #emph[Devoted healer:] you stay to treat a dying enemy and lose the chance to pursue their commander.

==== Beliefs and Compels
All three Belief categories use the same compel procedure. Acting in line with a Belief earns nothing by itself; only a complication that meets the standard above does. Several Beliefs behind one complication still earn one Tychē Point. Beliefs grant no modifiers and are not extra power tags.

#emph[Belief example:] your Self Belief rejects sacrificing people for advantage. You propose staying to escort stranded civilians instead of pursuing the fleeing saboteur. Before acceptance, agree that the delay will let the saboteur escape the immediate pursuit. Accepting that meaningful complication earns 1 Tychē Point; it does not dictate every action you take afterward.

Acting against a Belief is not refusing a compel and carries no automatic penalty (§12.3). An Other Belief never grants control over another PC.

=== 4.1 Refresh
- #strong[Base Refresh is 3.] At the start of each session, your Tychē Points reset to your Refresh (if you have more, keep them, up to the cap).
- #strong[Tychē cap.] You can hold at most #strong[Refresh + 3] Tychē Points. Any you would gain above the cap are lost.
- #strong[Spending Refresh.] Creation extras and ★ abilities cost Refresh (§3.6).
- An #strong[attuned artifact] (Module A) costs 1 Refresh while attuned, not merely while carried.
- #strong[Minimum Refresh is 1.] Purchases can't take Refresh below 1. Your current Tychē Points can still drop to 0.
- The advancement option \"+1 Refresh\" (§12) lets you buy Refresh back.

Refresh happens only at the start of a session, not at a new scene, exchange, Inquiry phase or downtime stay. It restores Tychē Points only; pools, injuries, injury trades and Disposition use their own procedures.

Design intent: power comes at the cost of narrative flexibility. A low-Refresh character is mechanically strong but has to accept compels to stay fuelled.
]
